body:
>     3. I think we should keep the "entangled" wording next to it, to keep it consistent with the segment on GC and the definition of entanglement. When the dis-entanglement would exactly take effect would indeed not be directly noticeable by script.

I can see that the ["Authors are strongly encouraged to explicitly close MessagePort objects to disentangle them"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html#ports-and-garbage-collection) advice would need to be adjusted if `close()` were not to disentangle.  I'm not finding a definition of entanglement that mentions close, but perhaps "[Disconnects the port"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html#message-ports) would seem a less apt description of `close()`. 

The disentangling on `close()` can have some unfortunate interactions with [`postMessage()`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html#dom-messageport-postmessage-options) and "transfer-receiving steps".  In the following example, disentangling on close and re-entangling in "transfer receiving steps" would mean that only 'after transfer received' would be logged, but I'd expect 'before transfer received' to also be logged, as is done in Firefox and Chrome at least.
```js
const mc = new MessageChannel;
const shipper = new MessageChannel;
shipper.port1.postMessage(null, [mc.port2]);
mc.port2.close(); // should this disentangle mc.port1 and mc.port2?
mc.port1.postMessage('before transfer received');
shipper.port2.onmessage = ({ports: [shipped]}) => {
  shipped.onmessage = e => console.log(e.data);
  mc.port1.postMessage('after transfer received');
};
```
However, this is an existing issue, which can be addressed separately.  I don't think the checks proposed here would introduce any undesirable interactions with (dis)entanglement; they would only improve the situation.
